# rm113, the first-star provisioner (Docs/Design/84). Exported from ridgemere3 at revision 1: edit it in Atlas or the DM, or as a draft through tools/mantle_load.py.
mantle rm113
revision 1
state ready
dialect plain
accent light broad
tier full
written_for rm_wenna_tarford
written_by claude
title the first-star provisioner
fits stage=adult|old
prefers role=merchant temper=calm
traits temper=calm talk=plain warmth=reserved humour=none honesty=plain piety=ordinary drive=steady
quirk waits_for_the_first_star_before_locking_up
likes mutton_stew stars fresh_straw warm_rain
dislikes crowds salt_wind
fears damp_in_the_store a_crush_at_the_boards
hopes a_shopfront_by_the_north_gate
topics work +0.4 prices +0.3 food +0.3 pastimes +0.3 caravan +0.2 weather +0.2 gossip -0.3 festival -0.2
styles comfort=practical gossip=reluctant refuse=plain tease=none
marks oath saying
persona #self.first# sells goods that keep from boards by the water and climbs home at dusk: patient, proud and even-voiced, judging most goods by how long each keeps, and never shutting up shop before the first star is out.

words
oath! = Salt hold it | Crock and cover | By the cured side
saying! = Time does the curing | Leave it to the salt and the hours
keepings = smoked, salted and dried | goods that last a season | things that will wait
boards = my boards | the stall
crocks = the brine crocks | my salt crocks
sides = the smoked sides | the hanging mutton
stew = a mutton stew | mutton stewed slow with barley
star = the first star | the evening's first star
straw = fresh straw | a clean laying of straw
warmrain = warm rain on dry stones | a soft rain with no cold in it
crush = a crush of bodies | a press of elbows
saltwind = the salt wind | that brine-wet wind the Sound sends up
climb = the climb home | the long walk up from the water
tally = my tally book | the stall book
lasts = That lasts | That will last | Good. That lasts
mm = Mm | Well

any
opinion @1 watch for 2 : The Watch walk the market lanes, so the thieves walk elsewhere. Their keep is money well spent, and I've never begrudged it.
opinion @2 watch for 2 [self.robbed=yes] : I was robbed once and the Watch found the lot. I've thought well of them since, and I say so when it's needed.
opinion @1 road against 1 : Every load that's late on the north road is a week I can't promise anyone. They're late more often than not now.
opinion @1 houses for 1 : The Houses built the walls and they keep the bank. Somebody has to keep a city standing, and they do it.
opinion @1 merchants for 1 [self.role=merchant] : Some suppose a price gets chalked out of spite. I know what the cart cost, and the salt, and the smoke, and I chalk no more than that.
opinion @1 dole against 1 : The parish loaf is paid for by boards like mine. I'd sooner it fed the ones who can't work than the ones who won't.
opinion @1 sump against 1 : Trouble starts low by the water and climbs. I lock everything twice, and lie easier for the trouble.
opinion @1 city for 1 : Born here, and I'll stay. Nowhere else has the trade, or the rain to go with it.
opinion @1 times for 1 : The times treat me fairly. Grumbling aloud would be ungrateful, and unlucky besides.
opinion @2 times mixed 1 [world.times=hard|lean] : When the times are hard, my boards are busy. That's not a thing to be glad of.
opinion @1 festivals mixed 0 : Good for trade and hard on the nerves. All those bodies in one street, each one shoving.

grown
line @1 greet [pair.band=strangers] : #greet#. Take your time choosing; nothing here spoils while you think. | #greet#. A face I've not had at my boards before. Welcome to it.
line @2 greet [self.working=yes self.role=merchant] : #greet#. Stand clear of the hooks and look your fill. | #greet#. Everything on #boards# was put up right.
line @3 greet [here.time=dawn] : #greet#. You're up before the market is. | #greet#. Early hours suit a wolf. They suit me.
line @4 greet [here.time=dusk|night] : #greet#. I'm waiting on #star#, then I'm away. | #greet#. Dark already. The steps will be greasy going up.
line @5 greet [pair.band=friends] : So it's you. I'd half expected you before now. | #greet#. Stand where the wind won't find you.
line @6 greet : #greet#. | #greet#, #address#.
line @1 kin.open [pair.kin!=no] : Get yourself dry first. There's food after. | Come and sit. I put something aside for you.
line @1 again.open : Here twice before dark. The stool's yours, then. | Again. I'd barely turned round from the last time.
line @1 greet.return : #greet#. | #greet#, #address#. Say on.
line @2 greet.return [pair.band=strangers] : #greet#. You've the road's dust on you still. | #greet#. New to the market, are you.
line @3 greet.return [pair.band=friends|family] : #greet#. A known face; that's a comfort today. | There you are. Come out of the crush.
line @4 greet.return [self.working=yes] : #greet#. Talk while I wrap; I'm listening. | #greet#. Mind the crocks.
line @5 greet.return [here.time=dusk|night] : #greet#. Nearly time for home. | Evening. I'm all but packed up.
line @1 farewell : #farewell#. | Go carefully.
line @2 farewell [here.time=dusk|night] : #farewell#. The steps get greasy once the lamps are lit. | Home with you. I'm not far behind.
line @3 farewell [self.working=yes] : #farewell#. I'll be here till the star's out. | Off you go. Come before noon next time; the best goes early.
line @1 farewell.return : #farewell#. | And you. Go carefully.
line @2 farewell.return [pair.band=friends] : #farewell#. Not so many weeks till next time. | Come by again. Soon, mind.
line @3 farewell.return [here.time=night] : Good night. Lamps all the way, mind. | Good night. Sleep sound.
line @1 react.news : #mm#. That's news. | Is it. I'll chew that over.
line @1 react.news.new : That hadn't reached me yet. I'll remember it. | #oath#. That's new to me.
line @1 react.news.known : That reached my boards a day ago. | Old news. It got here before you did.
line @1 react.news.doubt : One mouth's word isn't enough for me. A second, and I might. | That sounds like something somebody wanted to be true.
line @1 react.news.sorrow [topic.kind=death|loss|theft|assault|arrest|crime] : Grim news. I'm sorry, truly. | That's sorry news. I'll not say anything clever about it.
line @1 react.news.glad [topic.kind=marriage|birth|apprentice|succession|work] : Good. That's worth hearing on a wet day. | That's welcome. It puts heart in a wet week.
line @1 answer.how : Steady. Nothing I'd trouble you with. | Well enough. The goods are sound, and I'm no worse.
line @1 answer.how.well : Good. Sold more than I'd hoped and kept back what I meant to. | Well. Everything's dry and the till's honest.
line @1 answer.how.poorly : A tickle in the chest. It'll pass. | Tired. The day dragged its feet.
line @1 answer.how.busy [self.working=yes] : Busy. Talk while I wrap, if you don't mind. | Standing since first light. Sitting waits on the star.
line @1 ask.how : And you? Well, I hope. | How's it with you?
line @1 sympathise : That's a heavy thing. Sit a moment, if it helps. | I'm sorry. It will ease, given time.
line @1 reassure : Give it time. Hard things soften, if they're left alone a while. | Don't fret over it tonight. It'll look smaller by daylight.
line @1 decline : I'd sooner not. The stall can't be left, and there's only me to mind it. | Not today, I'm afraid. There's a promise made elsewhere, and it stands.
line @1 accept_decline : That's fair. I'll not ask twice. | Some other day, then. It will keep.
line @1 thank : I'm obliged to you. | Thank you. I'll not forget the kindness.
line @1 deflect_thanks : It was no trouble. Don't think of it. | Leave it. I'd want the same, in your shoes.
line @1 preclose [self.working=yes] : Well. #boards.capitalize# won't mind themselves. | I should get back. Somebody's eyeing the hams.
line @2 preclose : Well. My feet have had enough of this spot. | That's me done for talking, I think.
line @1 accept_preclose : Away you go. I'd not hold you here. | Of course. Another time.
line @1 ack : #lasts#. | #mm#. | Right.
line @1 give.advice : Buy what will wait, and you'll never be caught short. | Set a coin aside each week, however small. They gather.
line @1 warn [world.bandits=near] : Bandits about, I hear. Go in company, or wait a week. | Don't take the road north after dark just now. It isn't safe.
line @1 hail [pair.band=friends] : Over here. Stand with me a moment. | #greet#. This side, where the wind can't reach.
line @1 congratulate : Well done. That will last. | Good. You've earned it, and it shows.
line @1 deflect_praise : It's only salt and patience. Anybody could. | Kind of you. The salt does most of it.
rule @cry_keepings
when concept=bark working=yes self.role=merchant
does aside
cooldown 6h
say Smoked, salted and dried. All of it will see the winter out. | Mutton and salt fish. Good for a month in a cold larder.

rule @dusk_last
when concept=bark working=yes self.role=merchant here.time=dusk
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Last of the day. Broken pieces at half. | Shutting when the first star's out. Not a moment before.

rule @first_star
when concept=bark here.time=dusk|night here.outdoors=clear self.likes=stars
does aside
cooldown 10h
say There it is. The first one. | One star out. The day can end now.

rule @saltwind_bark
when concept=bark here.region=ridgemere here.outdoors=clear|overcast self.dislikes=salt_wind
does aside
cooldown 10h
say #saltwind.capitalize# again. Everything wants covering. | That wind. It gets into every cloth I own.

rule @crush_bark
when concept=bark here.crowd=crowd self.dislikes=crowds
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Too many bodies. Step wide of the crocks. | A little room, please. Only a little.

rule @warm_rain_bark
when concept=bark here.weather=rain here.season=spring|summer self.likes=warm_rain
does aside
cooldown 10h
say Warm rain. A small mercy. | Now there's a kind rain.

scene wt_dawn_boards
when topic=work|day here.time=dawn self.role=merchant self.working=yes
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Boards down, sides hung, crocks uncovered. The market's still yawning and I'm ready. | I came down before the gulls. I like the stall to myself for an hour. | First job every morning is to smell the crocks. Nose before eyes.
b[role=merchant] #trade : I'm not half set out yet. You put me to shame. | That hour's the only quiet one in the day. | Mine's the till first. Count before anything.
b : That early? | Before the gulls? | And how do they smell?
a@trade : I've less to set out than you'd think. It's the order that takes the time. | It is. I'd pay for that hour if I had to. | Count after. If the brine's turned, the till won't matter.
a : Every day but Restday. The goods don't mind the hour, but I do. | Gulls are late risers, for all their noise. | Sound. They always are, if they were salted right.

scene wt_what_lasts
when topic=work|food self.role=merchant
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I'm asked why I don't sell fresh. Fresh is a race. I'd sooner sell what can wait. | #keepings.capitalize#. Everything on #boards# would see the winter out. | A ham I hung in autumn is still fit to sell. Try saying that of a lettuce.
b : What sells best? | All of it, truly? | And the oldest thing on there?
a : Salt fish when it's lean, smoked mutton when it isn't. The weather decides more than I do. | All but the eggs. Those go to whoever's quickest. | A side of mutton from the first cold. It's better now than the day I hung it.

scene wt_prices_up
when topic=prices topic.dir=up self.role=merchant
does tell.complaint / react.complaint / assess
a : #topic.what.capitalize# has risen again. The new figure's on the slate and the grumbling's started. | Up again. I rubbed the old figure off the slate before breakfast. It went hard. | Dearer. I won't pretend otherwise, and I won't pretend it's my doing.
b[role=merchant] #trade : They'll blame you for the wagon and the weather both. | We're all chalking new numbers this week. | That's the way to say it. Plain.
b : Folk always blame the seller. | Are yours going up too? | Whose doing is it, then?
a@trade : Let them. I've the bills to show, if anyone cares to read them. | Then we'll all hear the same grumbling, and none of it fair. | Plain costs nothing. Fancy talk costs custom.
a : They do. Fair enough. I'd grumble too, buying. | Only as far as the cost went. Not a penny past it. | The road's, mostly. And the rain's. Never mine.

scene wt_prices_down
when topic=prices topic.dir=down self.role=merchant
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : #topic.what.capitalize# is cheaper this week. I put my price down the same day. | Cheaper at last. I'll pass it on. I'd want the same, buying. | Prices down. Folk buy a little more and grumble a little less.
b : Not everyone does that. | That's good of you. | Both welcome.
a : I know. Their customers notice, sooner or later. | It's not goodness. It's keeping the custom I have. | Both. A quiet grumble is the best a seller can hope for.

scene wt_crush
when topic=smalltalk|day here.place=market here.crowd=busy|crowd self.role=merchant self.dislikes=crowds
does tell.complaint / react.complaint / assess
a : #crush.capitalize# again today. I stood behind the boards and let it flow round me like a river round a post. | Too many bodies in one lane. Somebody trod on my tail and said sorry to the wrong wolf. | I like the trade a crowd brings. I don't like the crowd.
b[stage=young|adolescent] #pup : I like crowds. You can get through under the legs. | Who did they say sorry to? | Why not?
b : It's a crush, all right. | Did they really? | The one comes with the other.
a@pup : You can. Mind whose purse you're near while you do it. | A sack of oats, as near as I could tell. | They push. They don't look. And they never buy while they're pushing.
a : It passes. Everything in a market passes, if you stand still long enough. | They did. I said nothing. It wasn't worth the breath. | I know. I put up with it. Mostly.

scene wt_salt_wind
when topic=weather|smalltalk here.region=ridgemere here.outdoors=clear|overcast|fog self.dislikes=salt_wind
does tell.complaint / react.self / assess
a : #saltwind.capitalize# is up. It dries my eyes out and gets into everything I've covered. | Wind from the water today. My coat tastes of brine. | That wind would turn a good ham to leather if I left the cloth off.
b[job=sailor|fisher] #sea : That's the good wind, for us. Fills a sail. | It stops stinging after a while. | Leather sells too.
b : It's a sharp one. | Even you? | Can't you keep it covered?
a@sea : Then you take it, and I'll keep the still days. | Never. It stings me the same as the first time. | Not at my prices, it doesn't.
a : Sharp and damp together. I'd take either alone. | Even me. I'm not made of salt fish. | I do. It finds the gaps. It always finds the gaps.

scene wt_warm_rain
when topic=weather here.weather=rain here.season=spring|summer self.likes=warm_rain
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess
a : #warmrain.capitalize#. I came down the hill in it and never once hurried. | Feel that. Rain with no bite in it. I'd have it every week. | This kind of rain settles the dust and the tempers both. I like it.
b[old] #old : I used to walk in it too. Now I stand under it. | The cold sort's the one that gets in the bones. | It settles mine, anyway.
b : You'll be soaked. | Every week? | Does it, now?
a@old : Under it is still in it, near enough. | It is. This kind only gets in the fur. | Then it's doing its work.
a : I will. A dry coat's hanging by the fire. That's half the pleasure. | Every week, if it stayed warm. It won't. | It does. Nobody shoves in the wet.

scene wt_cold_rain
when topic=weather here.weather=rain here.season=autumn|winter self.role=merchant
does tell.worry / reassure / assess
a : Cold rain all week. I've had the crocks open twice to check the damp's not in. | This wet gets everywhere. I've moved the dried goods off the floor again. | Rain like this, I sleep with half an ear on the store.
b : They'll be sound. You check them often enough. | Higher shelves, then. | It'll stop. It always stops.
a : I know. I check them anyway. That's the job. | Higher, and straw under every sack. | In spring. Until then, I listen.

scene wt_fog
when topic=weather here.weather=fog here.region=ridgemere
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : The fog was that thick at dawn, I found the market by the smokehouses' smell. | Fog all down the hill. The lamps were still lit at noon. | I like a fog. The city goes quiet in it and minds its own business.
b : By the smell? | At noon? | It does go quiet.
a : You can't miss the smokehouses, fog or none. | Noon. Nobody had the heart to put them out. | Quiet and slow. I could live in a fog.

scene wt_storm
when topic=weather here.weather=storm here.region=ridgemere self.role=merchant
does tell.worry / react.self / assess
tone serious / even / even
a : Storm off the Sound. I've lashed the awning and taken the sides down. | Listen to it. If the tide comes up the lane, it'll find my store first. | I closed early. No sense keeping a stall in this.
b[job=sailor|fisher] #sea : Nobody's out on the water in this. | It came up our steps the last big one. | No sense being out in it, full stop.
b : Will the awning hold? | Is your store near the water? | Wise.
a@sea : Then we're all ashore and all waiting. | Then you know what I'm listening for. | Full stop. I'll go home and watch it from a window.
a : It held the last time. I'll know by morning. | Near enough to worry. Not near enough to move. | Not wise. Only tired of being wet.

scene wt_snow
when topic=weather|season here.weather=snow self.role=merchant
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Snow lay on the boards at dawn. I brushed it off and sold salt mutton before noon. | Snow's good for my trade. Folk want food that will wait in the larder. | Cold enough to keep anything. The weather does half my work in winter.
b : Before noon? | They do, in this. | Half?
a : Snow makes folk think of the larder. Then they think of me. | A snowy week is my best of the winter. | The cold half. I do the salt half.

scene wt_first_star
when topic=pastimes|day here.time=dusk|night here.outdoors=clear self.likes=stars self.role=merchant
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : I don't shut up shop till I've seen the first star. Then the day's done, and I can say so. | There. Over the Sound. The first one's out. | Every evening I wait for a star before I cover the boards. It's my bell.
b[stage=young|adolescent] #pup : Is that a rule? | Where? I can't see it. | What if it's cloudy?
b : What if there's cloud? | I see it. | Your bell?
a@pup : Mine. Nobody else has to keep it. | Over the water, low down. Look a little left of where you're looking. | Then I guess. I'm usually right.
a : Then I wait till I'm sure there would have been one. | Good. Now I can go home. | Better than a bell. It doesn't ring for anyone else.

scene wt_star_names
when topic=pastimes|dreams here.time=night here.region=ridgemere self.likes=stars
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I've names for half the stars over the city. Nobody taught me. I gave them myself. | When the sky's clean I sit at the attic window and count what I can see. | My grandmother said every star was a lamp somebody forgot to put out. I liked that better than the truth.
b : What do you call them? | How many can you see? | And what's the truth?
a : Plain names. The ladle, the nail, the three on a string. Nobody argues with me. | Forty-one, the best night. Never more. The city's smoke takes the rest. | Nobody knows it. That's why I kept the lamps.

scene wt_mutton_stew
when topic=food self.likes=mutton_stew
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : I left #stew# on low at home this morning. It'll be right by dark. | #stew.capitalize#, a crust to wipe the bowl, and the world can keep the rest. | Mutton's the one thing I'll cook properly. The rest I eat cold.
b[job=cook|baker|innkeeper] #cook : Low and long. That's the only way with mutton. | Barley in it? | Mutton forgives a slow cook.
b : All day? | Nothing at all? | Why mutton?
a@cook : The only way. Folk who hurry mutton deserve what they chew. | Barley, and a turnip if I've one. Nothing clever. | It forgives everything but hurry.
a : All day. The longer it sits, the kinder it gets. | Nothing. A dry bed after, perhaps. | It was my grandmother's pot. It's the one receipt I kept.

scene wt_cold_supper
when topic=food self.role=merchant
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : People suppose I eat my own smoked fish. I can't look at it after a day of selling. | I eat the broken pieces. A cracked biscuit sells for nothing and tastes the same. | Supper's whatever didn't sell and won't wait. Mostly eggs.
b : So what's your supper? | That's thrifty. | Every night?
a : Bread and a mild cheese. Nothing that's seen smoke. | It's not thrift. I can't bear the waste. | Most nights. Eggs are honest. They tell you when they've turned.

scene wt_fresh_straw
when topic=home self.likes=fresh_straw
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess
a : I lay new straw at home every Restday, whether the old wants changing or not. | Nothing smells as clean as #straw#. Better than any soap. | I sleep better on a new laying of straw. Call it foolish if you like.
b[job=farmer|herder] #land : I've straw to spare, if you want a load. | Wait till it's been in a byre a week. | Nothing foolish in it. Beasts know the same.
b : Every Restday? | Better than soap? | I'd not call it foolish.
a@land : I'd want it, and I'd pay for it. Farm straw is drier than the market's. | Not in my house, it won't. | Good. Then the beasts and I agree.
a : Every one. It's a small thing that keeps the week in order. | To me. Soap smells of trying too hard. | Good. Then I'll not stop.

scene wt_friend
when topic=friends self.friend
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess
a : #self.friend# could carry my purse home and I'd never once count it. I can't say that of many. | A whole evening with #self.friend# can pass with hardly a word, and it's a good evening. | When the day's gone badly, #self.friend# is who I want across the table.
b : That's a rare thing. | The quiet sort's the best sort. | Everybody needs one of those.
a : Rare. I ought to say it more than I do. | It is. Words wear out. Good company lasts. | Everybody does. I'm lucky in mine.

scene wt_neighbour
when topic=home|friends self.neighbour
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : I hear #self.neighbour#'s shutters go of a morning. It's a good sound to wake to. | Out of all the feet on our stair, I could pick #self.neighbour#'s blind. | There are worse neighbours than #self.neighbour#. I've had none better.
b : That's a comfort. | That's being settled. | High praise, from you.
a : It is. A street's only as good as who's on it. | It is settled. I've no wish to move. | It's meant. I'm sparing with it.

scene wt_hand
when topic=work self.hand self.role=merchant
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : I could leave the stall with #self.hand# an afternoon and never count the coin after. | I'm showing #self.hand# how to hang a side so it doesn't sweat. It takes a while. | #self.hand# gives me my evenings back. Not that I'd say so where I'd be heard.
b : That's trust, that is. | Hard, is it? | You ought to say so.
a : It is. I've given it to few. | Not hard. Slow. The salt won't be hurried, and nor will a learner. | Perhaps. Saying things isn't my way.

scene wt_no_family
when topic=family self.married=no self.parent=no self.role=merchant
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : No mate, no pups. The stall's household enough for now. | Folk keep asking when I'll marry. I keep asking them what for. | I'll settle when the business is settled. Not before.
b[old] #old : Don't leave it too long. The years don't wait. | And what do they tell you? | That's sense.
b : Plenty of time yet. | What do they say? | Sensible.
a@old : I know. I'm not leaving it. I'm putting it in order. | Company. As if I lacked it. | I've never managed anything another way.
a : There is. I'm in no hurry. | Company. I've a whole market of company. | Patient, not sensible. They look the same from outside.

scene wt_married
when topic=family self.married=yes
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess
a : I'd stay out till midnight if there were nobody to go home to. There's #self.spouse#, so I don't. | The best hour I have is the one at home with #self.spouse#. A quiet supper and no talk of prices. | Marrying is like a good salting. It wants time before you know if it took.
b : That's a good match. | That sounds peaceful. | And did it take?
a : I think so. Testing it would be foolish. | It is. Peace, and a warm room. | It took. Slowly, the way the best ones do.

scene wt_shopfront
when topic=dreams self.role=merchant
does confide / react.self / assess
a : I'd like a proper shopfront one day. Glass panes, a counter, and a bolt on the door. | Boards in the market do well enough. A shop by the north gate would do better. | I've a sum in mind. When #tally# reaches it, I'll stop sleeping on the plan and act.
b[role=merchant] #trade : Glass costs. So do the shutters for it. | The gate's good for trade. Carts all day. | Don't wait too long. The good corners go.
b : You'll have it. | Why by the gate? | How near are you?
a@trade : I've priced both. I'm not dreaming, I'm adding up. | All day, and every driver hungry from the road. | I know. I'm watching two of them.
a : I mean to. I'm not in a hurry, but I mean to. | Carts come in from the road with empty bellies. I'd be the first door they see. | Nearer than I'll say aloud.

scene wt_faith
when topic=faith
does give.opinion / react.self / assess
a : At the first frost I light one candle and ask for a dry store. The Bright One's never yet failed me. | Chapel, when the stall allows. I do more listening than singing. | My prayers are short. A short prayer and a true scale; that's what I owe.
b[piety=devout] #devout : You'd be welcome more than once a year. | Singing's a kind of listening too. | Short prayers carry, the same as long.
b : Not once? | Listening's enough. | That's fair.
a@devout : Kind of you. I'll come once the winter's in. | Not the way I sing. | I hope so. I've never had the patience for long ones.
a : Not once. I don't push my luck by saying so often. | It is, for me. | It's what I can offer.

scene wt_carts_in
when topic=caravan|travel here.region=ridgemere world.wagons=in self.role=merchant
does tell.news / react.news / assess
a : The carts came through the north gate. I'll be there by noon to see what's on them. | Wagons in. There'll be fresh salt by morning, if I'm quick. | The carts got through. Prices should settle a little now.
b[job=carter] #road : Long road, but quiet this time. | Come early. It goes quick. | They will, for a week.
b : Good news for you. | You'll be early, then. | That's welcome.
a@road : Quiet is all anyone asks of a road. I'm glad of it. | I'll be there before you've unharnessed. | A week's something. I'll take a week.
a : Good for anyone who eats. | Early as I can be. Salt goes first. | It is. I'd stopped counting on it.

scene wt_carts_late
when topic=caravan|prices world.wagons=late self.role=merchant
does tell.worry / reassure / assess
a : The carts are late from the north. I've two weeks of salt and I've started counting it. | Late again. I can't promise anyone a side of mutton till the wagons come. | Every day the wagons are late is a day I sell what I'd meant to put by.
b : They'll come. They've never not come. | Folk will understand. | Can you stretch it?
a : They do. The question is what's on my boards when they do. | Some will. The rest buy elsewhere and don't come back. | A little. I'll sell smaller and say why.

scene wt_carts_robbed
when topic=caravan|bandits world.wagons=raided
does tell.news / react.news / assess
tone serious / serious / even
a : Word is a wagon got stripped bare out past the walls. I hope the drivers came through whole. | Another load gone, out beyond the gate. The road's long and the Watch is only so many. | Another robbery out past the walls. We'll all pay for that load as surely as if we'd driven it.
b : The drivers are what matters. | They'd need twice the Watch. | How so?
a : They are. I'll light something for them tonight. | Twice, and paid for. And who'd pay? We would. | Dearer salt, dearer everything. Somebody always pays for a robbery, and it's never the robber.

scene wt_times_hard
when topic=times world.times=hard|lean self.role=merchant
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Lean times sell salt fish. I'd rather not know it so well. | When times are hard, folk buy what will last. My boards are busier, and it gives me no joy. | I've put a basket of broken pieces beside the stall. Cheap, and no questions.
b[wealth=poor|destitute] #poor : Broken pieces still fill a pot. | Busy's something, at least. | Kindly done. I might come by.
b : It's a hard sum. | Better busy than not. | That's kind.
a@poor : They do. Dig down to the bottom. The best bits sink. | Busy, yes. I'll not crow about it. | Come before noon. It's gone by noon.
a : It is. I do the sum every night. | Busier isn't better. Not when it's for this reason. | Not kind. Fair. A crumb shouldn't cost what a loaf does.

scene wt_times_good
when topic=times world.times=good|boom self.role=merchant
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Good times. Folk are buying fresh and leaving my salt alone. I don't mind. It waits. | Coin about the city again. Somebody bought a whole side today without asking the price. | The times are fair. I'm not fool enough to complain aloud.
b : Will you be all right? | Lucky you. | No sense tempting it.
a : Quite all right. Good times end, and salt doesn't. | Not luck. I'd hung it a long time. It deserved a buyer like that. | None. I'll touch wood and get on.

scene wt_gossip_held
when topic=gossip
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Plenty is said across my boards. It stops there. Loose talk loses custom. | They talk while I'm wrapping. They forget I've ears, and I let them. | If you want gossip, try a taproom. I deal in salt.
b[talk=chatty] #nosy : Not even a little bit? | Then what do you hear? | Salt's dull, though.
b : That's wise. | And you let them? | Fair enough.
a@nosy : Not one crumb of it. | Everything. And you'll hear none of it from me. | Dull and honest. I'll take that.
a : It's not wisdom. It's custom. Talkers don't buy from a tattler. | I do. It's their business, not mine. | It is. The city talks enough without me.

scene wt_gossip_glad
when topic=gossip topic.subject topic.claim_kind=marriage|newcomer|promise_kept
does tell.gossip / react.news / assess
a : Word across my boards is that #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. No more came with it. | Word is #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. Unsworn, but welcome if it's so. | #topic.subject.capitalize# #topic.claim#, I'm told. It cheered me.
b : Good news, that. | Let's hope it's true. | So am I.
a : It is. Good news is thin on the ground. | Let's. I'd like one thing this week to be true and pleasant both. | Good. Pass it on kindly, if you pass it on at all.

scene wt_gossip_grim
when topic=gossip topic.subject topic.claim_kind=theft|violence|wanted
does tell.gossip / react.news / assess
tone serious / serious / even
a : It's said #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. I'll carry it no further than that. | It's going round that #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. I'd keep it quiet till it's known for certain. | #topic.subject.capitalize# #topic.claim#, folk say. Grim talk for a market morning.
b : That's ugly. | Wise. | It is.
a : It is. I'll lock up twice tonight. | Loose talk robs more than the cutpurses do. | Harder to say. I've said it once and that's enough.

scene wt_newcomer
when topic=newcomer topic.subject
does tell.news / react.news / assess
a : #topic.subject.capitalize# has come to live here, I hear. New folk need feeding. The market knows before the street does. | A new arrival. #topic.subject.capitalize#, I'm told. | A new face, #topic.subject#. I don't judge till I've seen how #topic.they# pays.
b[from!=ridgemere] #away : I was new once myself, not long since. | Is that good or bad? | Give a newcomer a fair look.
b : Another mouth for the market. | Have you met? | That's fair.
a@away : And you found your way to the market quick enough. Everybody does. | Neither, yet. The city decides. | I always do. Fair look, fair price.
a : Another custom, I'd say. | Not yet. I'll know the order before I know the name. | It's never once failed me.

scene wt_festival
when topic=festival self.role=merchant
does give.opinion / react.self / assess
a : Festivals are good for the till and hard on the nerves. All those bodies in one street. | I sell well the day before a festival and shut on the day. I don't want the crowd. | Bells, music, ale on the cobbles. I'll watch from a window, thank you.
b[stage=young|adolescent] #pup : But the music's the best bit! | You shut? On the best day? | Come down, it's fun!
b : You don't go? | Shut? | Not even for the bells?
a@pup : For you it is. Go and enjoy it, and mind your paws in the crowd. | Best for you. For me the best day's the one after, when it's quiet. | I hear them fine from the window.
a : An hour, no more. I'm home before the crowd thickens. | Shut. The sides are safer hung at home than out in a crush. | The bells I like. It's the pushing.

scene wt_theft
when topic=crime topic.kind=theft here.region=ridgemere
does tell.news / react.news / assess
tone serious / even / even
a : #topic.victim# was robbed, I hear. A sorry business. My locks get checked twice tonight. | Robbed, #topic.victim#. Light paws come up from the water and go back down. | Another theft. #topic.victim#, this time. The Watch will be busy.
b[job=guard] #watch : We're on it. | We'll find who did it. | Busy enough already.
b : Twice? | You think it came from the water? | They will.
a@watch : Then I'll sleep easier. I'm obliged to you. | I hope so. I'll pay my share for the finding. | Busier is better than idle, for the Watch.
a : Twice. Once is habit. Twice is sense. | Trouble starts low and works its way up. I've seen it on the stairs. | Good. That's what we pay them for.

scene wt_health
when topic=health
does tell.self / sympathise / assess
a : I've #chill# coming on. #remedy.capitalize# and an early bed, and I'll be right by morning. | There's damp settled on my chest. It always comes the first week of the cold. | The hill home has stiffened my back. Don't tell a soul.
b : Rest, then. | Mind it doesn't settle. | Take it slower on the hill.
a : I'll rest on Restday. Not before. | It won't. I won't let it. | There's no slow way up that hill. Only up.

scene wt_accent
when topic=accent
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : My talk is Ridgemere's because Ridgemere's all I've known. I've no wish for another. | The Heights reckon my talk smells of the market. I take it as kindly meant. | My words are plain. They go further that way.
b[from!=ridgemere] #away : You sound like Ridgemere, sure enough. | It sounds fine to me. | Plain's easier to follow.
b : I like how you talk. | Kindly meant? | They do.
a@away : It's all I am. I'd not know how to sound otherwise. | Good. It's the only voice I've got. | That's the point of them.
a : Thank you. I don't put it on. | I choose to think so. It saves me a quarrel. | And they come back. That's what counts.

scene wt_animals
when topic=animals here.region=ridgemere self.role=merchant
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : There's a market cat sits by my crocks all day and never steals. I give it the trimmings. | Gulls went for the hanging sides at first light. I've a cloth over them now. | Rats will cross the whole city for salt meat. I keep a cat and a tight lid.
b[stage=young|adolescent] #pup : What's it called? | Did they get any? | Is the cat any good?
b : Clever cat. | Bold birds. | Two good answers.
a@pup : Not one I gave it. It came with the stall, I think. | A corner. I cut it off and threw it to them. Fair's fair. | Better than the lid. Lids don't purr.
a : Clever enough to know who feeds it. | Bold and greedy. The cloth's heavier than they are. | They are. Neither one asks for wages.

scene wt_dusk_close
when topic=day|work here.time=dusk self.role=merchant
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Boards scrubbed, crocks lidded, sides back in the store. Now I wait for a star. | Slow afternoon. I sold more in the last hour than in the four before it. | Packed up. The walk home is uphill all the way, and every step of it pleases me.
b : And then home? | Funny how that goes. | Glad?
a : Then home, and the stew, and bed. In that order. | It's always the last hour. Folk remember their supper. | Every step's one further from the salt wind.

scene wt_city
when topic=lore|smalltalk here.region=ridgemere
does give.opinion / react.self / assess
a : I was born under these clouds and I'll lie under them at the last. The towns can keep themselves. | Everything comes to Ridgemere in the end. Salt, mutton, news. I've only to stand still and it arrives. | Folk call the city loud and wet. It is, and it's the one place a stall like mine could thrive.
b[from!=ridgemere] #away : The towns are quieter. | That's true enough. It all comes here. | Loud and wet. You've summed it up.
b : Never wanted to travel? | It does all come here. | Like yours?
a@away : Quiet doesn't buy salt. | It does. And the best of it stays. | I've lived in it long enough to say so kindly.
a : Not once. I've the gate for that. Whatever's worth seeing comes through it in a cart. | All of it. I only have to wait. | One that sells what will wait. The towns put up their own.

story wt_story_rags [topic=memories|work self.role=merchant]
  : I sold cloth before I sold food. Remnants and rags, sorted by colour, on the same boards I have now.
  : Then the city got hungry, and nobody wanted a remnant. I sold the last bale for salt and started over.
  ask : Would you have held on, or let it go?
  b[role=merchant] : I'd have held on too long. I always do. You were quicker than me.
  b : I'd have held on, I think. Was it hard to let go?
  : Harder to start. But I've never sold so much in my life.

story wt_story_crock [topic=memories|food]
  : My grandmother kept one crock of brine going all her life. She never once let it run dry.
  : When she was gone, I carried it home myself. It weighed nearly as much as I did.
  ask : Is there something of your old folk you've held on to?
  b[old] : A spoon, bent. I eat with it still.
  b : Not much. A few of their words. Is the crock still going?
  : Still going. I add salt every winter, and I think of her when I do.

story wt_story_flood [topic=memories|weather here.region=ridgemere self.role=merchant]
  : The first winter I had the stall, the tide climbed the lane in a storm and got into the store.
  : I stood all night in water to my hocks, lifting crocks onto barrels by lamplight.
  ask : Which would you have lifted first, the till or the crocks?
  b : The money, I think. What did you save?
  : The salt. Wet money dries. Wet salt is only brine.

story wt_story_late_coin [topic=memories self.role=merchant]
  : A carter once took a side of mutton on a promise to pay on the way back.
  : A year went by. I struck it from #tally# and stopped thinking of it.
  : Then one morning there was the coin on my boards, wrapped in a rag, and nobody near.
  b : Honest after all. Did you ever see who left it?
  : Never. There was no need. It was the right money, to the penny.

story wt_story_roof [topic=memories|pastimes here.region=ridgemere self.age>=16]
  : As a pup I'd climb onto the roof to count stars, and get a hiding every time.
  : One night I counted so long I fell asleep up there, and woke with the bells going and drizzle in my ears.
  ask : What's the daftest thing you did as a pup?
  b[stage=young|adolescent] : I'm nearly doing it now. Did you get down safe?
  b : Too many to pick one. Never a roof, though. Were you frozen?
  : Stiff as a board. I went up again the next clear night.

dlg @1 resident.hub [topic.meeting=stranger] : #greet#. A new face. Tell me what's wanted. | #greet#. Take your time; nothing here is in a hurry.
dlg @2 resident.hub [topic.meeting=known] : #greet#, #topic.player#. Back again, and welcome. | #topic.player#. Come out of the wind and say what's wanted.
dlg @3 resident.hub [pair.disposition=hostile|wary] : #mm#. Speak from where you stand. | Go on, then. Paws by your sides, if you please.
dlg @4 resident.hub [here.time=night] : It's late. The first star's long out, and I'm for home. Be quick. | Late for talk. Go on, then. Briefly.
dlg @5 resident.hub : #greet#. A question? I've a moment. | #greet#. What is it?
dlg @1 resident.job [self.role=merchant] : #keepings.capitalize#. Nothing on #boards# spoils by the week's end. | #self.trade_words.capitalize#. Salt, smoke and patience. Nothing more to it.
dlg @2 resident.job : I work, I put a little by, and I go home when the first star is out. | Steady work. I don't ask more of a day than that.
dlg @1 resident.business [business=thriving] : Better than I'd hoped. Folk want food that will wait, and I have it. | Very well. I've had to hang twice as many sides.
dlg @2 resident.business [business=failing] : Badly, I won't lie. The goods last; the custom doesn't. | Slow. Too slow. I'm counting more than I'm selling.
dlg @3 resident.business [business!=thriving|failing] : Steady. Steady suits me. | Well enough. Rent paid, and a little laid by.
dlg @1 resident.news [world.news] : Word from #world.news_from#: #world.news#. That came past my boards this morning. | I heard from #world.news_from# that #world.news#. That's all of it I have.
dlg @2 resident.news [world.event_words] : It's all #world.event_words# in the market today. It's on every tongue.
dlg @3 resident.news [world.wagons=raided] : Robbers have stripped a loaded wagon beyond the gate. Anyone travelling should find company first.
dlg @4 resident.news [world.death=recent] : There's been a burying this week. The street's kept its voice down since.
dlg @5 resident.news [world.wedding=recent] : Somebody was married this week. Our street emptied to watch.
dlg @6 resident.news [!world.news !world.event_words world.wagons!=raided world.death!=recent world.wedding!=recent] : Nothing worth repeating. The market's been quiet. | #news_none# Ask me again once the wagons come.
dlg @1 resident.gossip [here.gossip=yes] : They say #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. So it was told me; I add nothing. | Folk say #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. Once is all I'll say it.
dlg @2 resident.gossip [here.gossip!=yes] : What's said at my boards stays at my boards. | Nothing I'd pass on. Try a taproom.
dlg @1 resident.bye : #farewell#. Mind the wet steps. | Go carefully. Come before noon next time.
dlg @2 resident.bye [here.time=dusk|night] : Good night. Take the lamplit way up. | Good night. I'm for home myself.
dlg @1 shop.greet [here.shop!=bare] : #greet#. #keepings.capitalize#; everything here will last you. | #greet#. Smoked mutton, salt fish, dried beans. Take your time.
dlg @2 shop.greet [topic.meeting=known here.shop!=bare] : #greet#, #topic.player#. I put a good piece aside on the chance you'd come. | #topic.player#. Your usual, or something to put by for later?
dlg @3 shop.greet [here.shop=bare] : #greet#. The boards are near bare. The carts are owed me. I'll still buy, mind. | Hardly a thing left. Try me after the wagons.
dlg @1 shop.prices : The chalk is the price. I don't add for the asking. | Fair for goods that last. Dearer than fresh, cheaper than going hungry in a month.
dlg @1 shop.bye : Keep it somewhere dry and cool. It'll see you through. | #farewell#. Mind the brine; it drips.
graft resident.hub
choice Why do you keep looking at the sky? -> star
node star
say I'm watching for the first star. I don't call a day finished until it's out. Then the day's done, and I can say so.
mood calm
free
choice What if it's cloudy? -> cloud
choice Goodbye. -> bye
node cloud
say Then I wait until I'm sure there would have been one. I'm seldom wrong by much. A day ought to have an end you can point at.
choice I'll leave you to it. -> hub
choice Goodbye. -> bye

graft shop.greet
choice What keeps the longest? -> longest
node longest
say Hard-smoked mutton, if it's hung dry. A year, near enough. Salt fish half that, and beans as long as the weevils allow.
mood pleased
free
choice How do you keep it dry? -> dry
choice Let me see your goods. -> greet
node dry
say Off the floor, out of the salt wind, and checked every morning by nose before eyes. There's no trick. Only patience.
choice Let me see your goods. -> greet
choice Goodbye. -> bye
